              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The acetylcholine that is released by the motor nerves at the neuromuscular junction binds to NICOTINIC receptors. (In other words, the neurotransmitter that moves your muscles binds to nicotinic receptors)</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The acetylcholine that is released at the effector in the parasympathetic nervous system (PNS), binds to MUSCARINIC receptors. (In other words, the main receptors for Acetylcholine in the PNS are the muscarinic receptors.)</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>NO,&nbsp;This one is incorrect. Because of the fact that the receptors in the PNS are completely different to the receptors in the muscles, we can make drugs that affect the PNS without affecting movement. This one is incorrect. Because of the fact that the receptors in the PNS are completely different to the receptors in the muscles, we can make drugs that affect the PNS without affecting movement. </div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "This one is incorrect. Because of the fact that the receptors in the PNS are completely different to the receptors in the muscles, we can make drugs that affect the PNS without affecting movement. \n"
                  },
                  "text": "<div>When we give a drug that affects the Parasympathetic Nervous System, it always affects muscle movement.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                }
              ],
